/// Behavioral API documentation for the Ledgerly backend.
///
/// A static Markdown document describing the current public API: purpose,
/// authentication, authorization, identity derivation, units, encoding,
/// lifecycle, polling, mutation retry safety, errors and non-obvious gotchas.
/// It reads no state, so it is a plain `query` function.
mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# Ledgerly Backend API\n\nLedgerly is a personal financial planning and execution system. The backend stores each user's income and expense entries, budget allocation, funding projects, business finances, debts and payments, and computes reconciled reports, spending alerts and portable backups. All amounts are money in minor units (cents) kept exact as signed integers; all timestamps are signed nanoseconds since the Unix epoch (`Time.now()`).\n\n## Authentication and identity\n\nEvery method that reads or writes financial data requires a signed (non-anonymous) caller. The app's frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available; an agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires.\n\nRegistration gates access. A direct API caller must register once by calling `_initialize_access_control` as a signed-in caller before any role-guarded call, including guarded queries. The first initializer receives the `#admin` role; every subsequent caller receives `#user`. An unregistered or anonymous caller receives a trap on guarded endpoints, for example `Unauthorized: Only users can perform this action` or `User is not registered`. A caller can be unregistered while assuming the app already knows it: registration happens only when a caller signs in through the app's own frontend, so a principal that never did so is unregistered even when it belongs to the app's owner, and a signed-in caller derived against a different origin is a different principal than the one the frontend registered.\n\n## Authorization\n\nAll financial records are private per user. Every collection is keyed by owner principal, and every domain operation is scoped to the calling principal: a caller can only ever read or modify its own entries, budgets, projects, business records, debts and payments. Another owner's record id is reported as `#notFound`. The OQL `schema()` and `execute()` endpoints apply the same per-user scoping: a signed-in caller sees only its own rows, while the platform controller sees all rows.\n\n## Units and encoding\n\n- Money: `Int` minor units (cents). Negative values are valid (balances, cash flow, remaining funding).\n- Timestamps: `Int` nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.\n- Record ids: `Nat`, allocated from a per-owner counter and never reused.\n- `?Float` interest rates are optional; `null` means unspecified.\n- Variants: `EntryKind` (`#income`/`#expense`), `BudgetPeriod` (`#daily`/`#weekly`/`#monthly`/`#annual`), `BusinessKind` (`#revenue`/`#cost`), `AlertSeverity` (`#info`/`#warning`/`#critical`).\n- Fallible operations return `LedgerlyResult<T>` = `#ok(T)` or `#err(LedgerlyError)`.\n\n## Methods\n\n### Entries\n- `createEntry(kind, amount, date, category, note)` - create an income or expense entry. Amount must be positive and category non-empty.\n- `updateEntry(id, kind, amount, date, category, note)` - update one of the caller's entries.\n- `deleteEntry(id)` - delete one of the caller's entries.\n- `listEntries(filter)` - list the caller's entries, most recent first, with optional `from`/`to`/`kind`/`category` filters.\n\n### Budgets\n- `setBudgetSettings(expectedIncome, period)` - set expected income and reporting period; recalculates every category allocation. Rejected with `#overAllocated` when existing categories would exceed 100 percent.\n- `getBudgetSettings()` - the caller's settings, or `null`.\n- `createBudgetCategory(name, percentage)` - create a category taking a percentage of expected income. Rejected with `#overAllocated` when the total would exceed 100 percent or the expected income.\n- `updateBudgetCategory(id, name, percentage)` - update a category; recalculates its allocation.\n- `deleteBudgetCategory(id)` - delete a category.\n- `listBudgetCategories()` - the caller's categories with allocations derived from current expected income.\n\n### Projects\n- `createProject(name, target, description)` / `updateProject(projectId, name, target, description)` / `getProject(projectId)` / `listProjects()` - project CRUD.\n- `addContribution(projectId, amount, date, note)` / `listContributions(projectId)` - contributions toward a project.\n- `addProjectExpense(projectId, amount, date, category, note)` / `listProjectExpenses(projectId)` - expenses charged against a project. Project expenses are stored only in the project-expense collection and are never written into personal entries or business records, so they are not double-counted in personal or business reports.\n- `getProjectFunding(projectId)` - target, contributed, spent and remaining funding for a project.\n\n### Business\n- `addBusinessRecord(kind, amount, date, category, note)` / `updateBusinessRecord(id, ...)` / `deleteBusinessRecord(id)` / `listBusinessRecords(from, to)` - business revenue and cost records, stored separately from personal entries.\n- `getBusinessSummary(from, to)` - revenue, costs, profit and cash flow computed from business records only.\n\n### Debt\n- `createDebt(name, balance, interestRate)` / `updateDebt(debtId, name, balance, interestRate)` / `deleteDebt(debtId)` - debt CRUD. Deleting a debt also removes its recorded payments.\n- `listDebts()` - the caller's debts with remaining balances and total outstanding.\n- `recordDebtPayment(debtId, amount, date, note)` - record a payment; the debt's balance is reduced by the payment amount. A payment larger than the outstanding balance is rejected with `#invalidAmount`.\n- `listDebtPayments(debtId)` - the caller's payments, optionally filtered to one debt.\n\n### Reports, alerts and backup\n- `getFinancialReport(period, from, to)` - a reconciled report over an inclusive nanosecond window, with income, expenses, net and per-category expense totals computed straight from the stored entries.\n- `getSpendingAlerts(from, to)` - spending alerts derived from actual recorded data: one per budget category whose spending approaches or exceeds its allocation, plus a total-spending alert against expected income.\n- `exportBackup()` - a portable snapshot of the caller's own records.\n- `restoreBackup(data)` - replace the caller's collections from a snapshot. Every restored record is owned by the caller, and id counters advance past the restored ids so new records never collide.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\nAll read methods are `query` calls and return immediately. All mutations are update calls. There is no asynchronous job or polling loop: a mutation's effect is visible to the next read. Reports and alerts are computed on demand from current state, so a client re-reads them after a mutation rather than polling.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\nCreate operations allocate a fresh id per call, so retrying a create after an ambiguous failure can produce a duplicate record. Update and delete operations are idempotent by id: re-issuing an update with the same arguments yields the same record, and re-issuing a delete of an already-deleted id returns `#notFound`. `restoreBackup` is destructive: it replaces the caller's entire set of collections, so a retry re-applies the same snapshot. `recordDebtPayment` reduces the debt balance each time it succeeds, so a retried payment is applied twice; confirm the first call's outcome before retrying.\n\n## Errors and limits\n\n- `#notAuthorized` - the caller may not perform the operation.\n- `#notFound(id)` - the record does not exist or is not owned by the caller.\n- `#invalidAmount(text)` - a non-positive or out-of-range amount.\n- `#overAllocated({ allocated; expectedIncome })` - a budget change would allocate more than expected income.\n- `#invalidPercentage(text)` - a percentage outside 0 to 100.\n- `#invalidInput(text)` - a missing or malformed field.\n\nGuarded endpoints trap for anonymous or unregistered callers. Amounts are exact integers; clients should format minor units for display and never round-trip through floating point.\n";
  };
};
